tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Obelisk quarrying and raising", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "The Unfinished Obelisk in its trench with dolerite pounders, and one being tipped into a sand pit.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Egypt (New Kingdom), c. 1500-1200 BCE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Single-piece granite monoliths of up to 30 metres and 300 tonnes were pounded out of the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Aswan bedrock, floated downriver on barges, and tipped upright into a sand-filled pit.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Workers pounded trenches around the shaft with balls of dolerite (harder than granite),", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "undercut it, and levered it free. The Unfinished Obelisk at Aswan (abandoned when it", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "cracked) shows the method; Hatshepsut's reliefs at Deir el-Bahari show a pair carried on", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "one huge barge. Raising probably used an embankment and a sand-filled funnel pit that was", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "slowly emptied so the shaft rotated onto its base.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:egypt/memphis", "color": "aqua"}
